In its statement of claim Monarch alleges that:
Messrs Puxty and Coggan breached various provisions of their respective employment agreements including, among others, in relation to restraint of trade and confidentiality;
Messrs Puxty and Coggan breached their fiduciary duties owed to Monarch and contravened s 182(1) and s 183(1) of the Corporations Act;
Odyssey was involved in the alleged breaches by Messrs Puxty and Coggan set out in (2) above;
the respondents made representations to clients of Monarch which were not true and, in doing so, they engaged in conduct which was misleading or deceptive or likely to mislead or deceive in contravention of s 18 of the Australian Consumer Law being Sch 2 to the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth) (ACL); and
the respondents engaged in conduct which wrongly suggested a connection with the services provided by Monarch and which amounted to passing off.

In her first affidavit sworn on 23 December 2020 Ms Coulter puts into evidence:
a balance sheet for Monarch showing Monarch’s net asset position as at 31 December 2018, 31 December 2019 and 31 December 2020;
business activity statements (BAS) for the nine quarters between 1 July 2018 and 30 September 2020. Ms Coulter notes that the total revenue in the BAS for the period 1 October 2019 to 30 September 2020 is $416,861;
profit and loss statements for the financial year ended 30 June 2020 and for the period 1 May 2020 to 31 October 2020, noting that the gross profit for the former period was $404,163.66 and for the latter period was $184,606.17; and
a copy of a bank statement for an account in the name of Monarch held with Macquarie Bank showing a closing balance as at 10 December 2020 of $73,447.18.

In a subsequent affidavit sworn on 18 February 2021 Ms Coulter gives evidence about the sale of Monarch’s business. She says that this evidence was not included in her first affidavit because settlement of the relevant business sale agreement had not yet occurred. Her evidence is as follows:
in or about December 2020 Monarch entered into a non-binding term sheet with Newlane Risk Pty Ltd (Newlane) for the sale of its proprietary assets including goodwill and the Monarch trail book and business records (Term Sheet);
the Term Sheet set out the essential terms of the agreement to sell Monarch’s assets to Newlane. In accordance with the Term Sheet Newlane agreed to pay the initial sum of $5,000 as a holding deposit and, on 10 December 2020, Ms Coulter in fact received the holding deposit;
on 21 December 2020, in her capacity as director of Monarch, Ms Coulter executed and exchanged a business sale agreement with Newlane. The purchase price for the sale of the Monarch business is made up as follows:
an initial cash payment of $504,000 inclusive of the holding deposit;
the deferred purchase price calculated subject to certain criteria and payable approximately 15 months from the date of the business sale agreement; and
an incentive payment payable approximately 15 months from the date of the business sale agreement,
(collectively, Purchase Price);
Newlane was instructed to deposit the Purchase Price and the holding deposit into a bank account in Ms Coulter’s own name. Ms Coulter intended for those funds to remain in an offset account for her mortgage;
on 2 February 2021 the business sale agreement settled and Ms Coulter received the sum of $499,000 being the remainder of the initial cash payment (see (3)(a) above);
between 12 February 2021 and 16 February 2021 Ms Coulter attended to two separate transfers in the amount of $95,000 from her own account to Monarch’s account. She undertook the transfers in two transactions because her personal account is subject to daily transfer limits; and
as at 17 February 2021 there was a balance of $201,621.96 in Monarch’s bank account held with Macquarie Bank.
